"""Evidence-linked manuscript edits; bibliography and protected literature remain in source OOXML."""
import json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
S=json.loads((ROOT/'tables/summary.json').read_text())
P={
7: '''Hereditary breast and ovarian cancer multigene testing leaves many germline missense variants unresolved. We developed HBOCpred as a research prioritisation ensemble that reports four calibrated learner probabilities, their vote vector, V0–V4 agreement states and a continuous S_MAC ranking score. The same descriptors apply to every evaluable record: unanimous B/LB-directed votes at V0, mixed votes at V1–V3 and unanimous LP/P-directed votes at V4; none assigns ACMG/AMP status. Development used 2,160 source-labelled variants from 17 genes, with a separate analysis restricted to 2,120 variants having a current missense annotation. In five repeats of nested five-fold cross-validation, all preprocessing and feature selection were fitted within training partitions. The annotation-completeness rule permitted outputs for 2,046/2,160 anchors per repeat. Among these, the secondary ≥3-of-4 endpoint achieved balanced accuracy 0.9604 ± 0.0023; S_MAC yielded AUROC 0.9909 ± 0.0013 and Brier score 0.0294 ± 0.0010. Values after ± describe split stability. In repeat 1, 1,968/2,160 anchors had unanimous votes, with 1,920/1,968 agreeing with the source label; 32 LP/P anchors nevertheless received V0. Leave-one-gene-out AUROC was 0.9812 among 2,045 evaluable anchors, with substantial gene-specific variation. Among 43,679 unresolved records, V0, V1–V3 and V4 contained 33,414, 4,491 and 5,774 variants. These are internal source-label consistency estimates, subject to predictor–label dependence and distribution shift. Independent clinical performance and utility remain unestablished.''',
16: '''We therefore developed and internally evaluated HBOCpred to support expert research review of unresolved variants in a defined 17-gene study panel. The model combines two conventional non-tree learners, a bagging learner and a boosting learner, retaining their calibrated probabilities, complete four-bit vote vector, V0–V4 agreement state and continuous S_MAC ranking score. An identical reporting rule is used for source-labelled anchors and unresolved application records: V0 denotes unanimous B/LB-directed votes, V1–V3 mixed votes and V4 unanimous LP/P-directed votes. These descriptors concern model direction and disagreement, not clinical certainty. We evaluated source-label consistency, annotation completeness, gene-specific generalisability and development-to-application shift; the outputs do not establish ACMG/AMP classifications, PP3/BP4 evidence strengths or clinical posterior probabilities.''',
19: '''The source spreadsheets yielded 90 numeric fields after SpliceAI aggregation and removal of its positional fields. A label-independent semantic reduction produced 48 candidate representatives; Reliability_index was excluded from the primary analysis, leaving 47 candidates. Missingness filtering, imputation, scaling, supervised consensus ranking and correlation pruning were refitted separately in every inner-training and outer-training partition. No application-archive missingness filter was used. The deployment refit retained 25 predictors, of which 22 appeared in at least 20 of 25 outer-training panels (Table 3). This is a new reanalysis of the source inputs; its fitted objects and estimates supersede those in the previous revision.''',
20: 'Table 3. Deployment panel and selection frequency across 25 outer-training fits.',
21: '2.2 Fixed learner families and repeated nested internal evaluation',
22: '''The revision evaluated four fixed learner families: SVM–RBF, L2 logistic regression, Extra Trees and histogram gradient boosting. Their hyperparameters, calibrators and vote thresholds were fitted within each training partition; there was no new nine-learner selection competition. Deployment vote thresholds were 0.5000, 0.6897, 0.5905 and 0.5000, respectively (Table 4). The learner set and reporting terminology were revision-stage choices informed by earlier development, so this analysis does not constitute an independent validation of those choices.''',
23: 'Table 4. Fitted deployment hyperparameters, calibration coefficients and vote thresholds.',
25: '''Across five complete outer-test repeats, 2,046/2,160 anchors (94.72%) satisfied the annotation-completeness rule in each repeat. Among evaluable anchors, the secondary ≥3-of-4 endpoint achieved balanced accuracy 0.9604 ± 0.0023, sensitivity 0.9457 ± 0.0021 and specificity 0.9751 ± 0.0037. Mean PPV, NPV, F1 and MCC were 0.9699, 0.9549, 0.9576 and 0.9228. S_MAC yielded AUROC 0.9909 ± 0.0013, AUPRC 0.9912 ± 0.0013 and Brier score 0.0294 ± 0.0010 (Figure 2). The ± values are sample standard deviations across repeat-level estimates, not confidence intervals. All metrics measure agreement with the processed source labels in the evaluable subset.''',
26: '''The 114 nonevaluable anchors comprised 80 B/LB and 34 LP/P records; their learner probabilities, votes, V-states and S_MAC were suppressed and they remained in source-cohort accounting. Thus, discrimination and binary metrics do not describe every source record. Outer-test calibration slopes ranged from 0.968 to 1.036 and intercepts from −0.148 to −0.023. These calibration results concern the ascertained source-label distribution and cannot establish clinical probability calibration.''',
27: '''In the fixed first-repeat out-of-fold audit, V0 contained 1,078 anchors (1,046 B/LB and 32 LP/P), V1–V3 contained 78 (45 B/LB and 33 LP/P), and V4 contained 890 (16 B/LB and 874 LP/P); 114 were incomplete (Table 5). Unanimous-vote coverage was 1,968/2,160 (91.11%), with source-direction agreement in 1,920/1,968 unanimous outputs (97.56%). Opposite unanimous direction occurred for 32/973 LP/P anchors at V0 (3.29%; Wilson 95% interval, 2.34–4.61%) and 16/1,187 B/LB anchors at V4 (1.35%; 0.83–2.18%). These proportions use all source records of the respective label; their rate ratio was 2.44.''',
29: '''Figure 2. Internal evaluation of the refitted ensemble. (A) Selection frequency of the deployment predictors across 25 outer-training panels. (B) S_MAC ROC curves for five outer-test repeats. (C) Precision–recall curves; the baseline is the LP/P proportion among the evaluable anchors. (D) Decile calibration curves. Each repeat includes 2,046 evaluable anchors and excludes 114 records failing the same output-completeness rule used in application. Repeated curves describe split stability on this source cohort and do not represent external validation.''',
30: 'Table 5. First-repeat out-of-fold vote groups and incomplete-record accounting.',
31: '''Note. V0 and V4 denote unanimous direction of four correlated model votes; V1–V3 denotes disagreement. The terminology is identical for anchors and application records. Incomplete is an annotation-quality flag, not a clinical class. Unanimity coverage uses all 2,160 source anchors; conditional agreement uses the 1,968 unanimous outputs. Neither is an accuracy estimate for unresolved variants.''',
32: '2.3 Shared output descriptors and vote-pattern audit',
33: '''The deployment model assigned the 43,679 unresolved records to V0 (33,414), V1 (1,943), V2 (1,189), V3 (1,359) and V4 (5,774). All satisfied the completeness requirement. The shared descriptors therefore comprised 33,414 unanimous B/LB-directed, 4,491 mixed-vote and 5,774 unanimous LP/P-directed records (Figure 3). V0 is not a benign classification or an instruction to discontinue review, and V4 is not a pathogenic classification. The exact state, learner identities and probabilities remain available with S_MAC.''',
34: '''Among evaluable first-repeat anchors, mixed votes occurred in 78/2,046 (3.81%), compared with 4,491/43,679 (10.28%) application records, a 2.70-fold difference. This comparison conditions on evaluability in both datasets; the 114 incomplete anchors are reported separately. It is a difference in model-output distributions, not evidence that the unresolved variants have a particular true-label error rate.''',
35: '''The first-repeat mixed-vote states contained 27 V1 anchors (9 LP/P), 25 V2 anchors (11 LP/P) and 26 V3 anchors (13 LP/P). The LP/P fractions were 44.0% at V2 (Wilson 95% interval, 26.7–62.9%) and 50.0% at V3 (32.1–67.9%). These overlapping intervals do not support ordered clinical tiers. The earlier V2/V3 point-ordering reversal belonged to the previous fit and is not reproduced as a result of this reanalysis.''',
36: '''At exact 2:2 disagreement, the two tree learners supplied the LP/P-directed votes in 16/25 anchors (10 LP/P and 6 B/LB). One tree and one conventional learner formed the pair in 9/25 (1 LP/P and 8 B/LB); the two conventional learners never formed the pair. Pair identity describes structured algorithmic disagreement and is not a biological class.''',
38: '''Figure 3. Shared vote states and completeness accounting. (A) First-repeat evaluable anchor counts by V-state and source label. (B) Deployment counts among 43,679 unresolved records. (C) Vote groups and incomplete records as percentages of all anchors within each source label. (D) Identities of the two LP/P-directed learners at V2; colours indicate source labels. The same directional descriptors are used throughout, without uncertainty-low/mid/high labels or clinical reclassification.''',
39: '2.4 Sensitivity analyses',
40: '''Five-fold sensitivity analyses used the first-repeat fold assignments. Including Reliability_index yielded balanced accuracy 0.9614 and AUROC 0.9891, versus 0.9592 and 0.9886 in the primary first repeat. Excluding both BayesDel addAF and AlphaMissense, while retaining the other eligible candidates, yielded 0.9607 and 0.9896. These small within-cohort differences do not establish equivalence or remove predictor–label dependence. Refitting on the 2,120 variants with a current same-gene transcript missense annotation yielded balanced accuracy 0.9615 and AUROC 0.9902 among 2,019 evaluable records; restricting the primary predictions to the same records yielded 0.9584 and 0.9884 (Table 6). In a separate descriptive subset of 1,998 anchors whose embedded ClinVar significance agreed with the processed source label, mean balanced accuracy was 0.9598 ± 0.0022 and AUROC 0.9907 ± 0.0013; this was a prediction-subset analysis, not a new external sample.''',
41: 'Table 6. Internal sensitivity analyses with explicit total and evaluable denominators.',
42: '2.5 Gene-held-out evaluation and reference-source audits',
43: '''Seventeen leave-one-gene-out fits each excluded one complete gene from preprocessing, selection, tuning, calibration and threshold estimation. Pooled across 2,045 evaluable anchors, the ≥3-of-4 endpoint had balanced accuracy 0.9453, sensitivity 0.9276 and specificity 0.9629; S_MAC AUROC was 0.9812, AUPRC 0.9810 and Brier score 0.0443. There were 115 incomplete records. Figure 4 and the accompanying per-gene table report the denominators, confusion counts, vote states and Wilson intervals for every gene.''',
44: '''Aggregate performance concealed important variation. For FANCA, sensitivity was 50/75 (66.7%) among evaluable LP/P anchors; for NBN it was 0/2, with a correspondingly wide interval. BRCA1 sensitivity was 193/207 (93.2%) and specificity 306/321 (95.3%); BRCA2 sensitivity was 77/81 (95.1%) and specificity 289/302 (95.7%). PTEN specificity of 5/5 must be read with its small evaluable benign denominator and 17 incomplete benign anchors. These are gene-specific internal source-label results, not evidence of uniformly transferable clinical performance.''',
45: 'The complete hospital source list contained 122 rows, of which 47 fell within the 17-gene scope. These 47 rows resolved to 31 distinct missense variants after transcript aliases and repeated records were collapsed (Table 7). Twenty-two variants overlapped the anchor set, eight were in the frozen application catalogue, and one was present only in the wider source training archive. All 31 met the ≥20/25 completeness rule and were scored with the frozen deployment model. Anchor-overlapping outputs are in-sample illustrations, not independent validation. CHEK2 Ile157Thr received V0 (S_MAC = 0.0093), illustrating why model direction cannot replace clinical interpretation. No hospital reference labels were adjudicated or external performance estimates calculated.',
46: 'The gnomAD eligibility audit identified 50 missense candidates: 47 overlapped the full training archive and one additional candidate overlapped the unresolved archive. The two remaining candidates, in CHEK2 and PTEN, were missense on alternative transcripts but not on MANE Select and lacked complete aligned model inputs. Neither was scored. This screen therefore supplied no independent benign test set (Supplementary Figure S1).',
48: '''Figure 4. Per-gene leave-one-gene-out evaluation. Points show sensitivity and specificity of the secondary ≥3-of-4 endpoint; lines show Wilson 95% intervals. Row labels give the evaluable LP/P and B/LB denominators. Each gene was absent from every fitting stage of its corresponding model. Incomplete records are excluded from these fractions and retained in the supplementary per-gene accounting. Perfect point estimates with small denominators should not be interpreted as established perfect performance.''',
50: '''Matched-record comparisons with REVEL, MetaLR, VARITY_R, BayesDel addAF and AlphaMissense were descriptive because these scores were eligible or retained model inputs. Across repeats, S_MAC AUROC was approximately 0.9906–0.9909 on the respective matched subsets, compared with 0.8773–0.9630 for the native scores (Figure 5). This comparison cannot establish superiority against independent tools. For the secondary ≥3-of-4 endpoint, stable rescue required a correct ensemble direction and an incorrect comparator direction in all five outer-test appearances. Each comparator threshold was estimated using only its outer-training records.''',
51: '''The review-status audit explicitly separates source counts from evaluable counts (Figure 6). The NR category contains 101 records, comprising 78 B/LB and 23 LP/P, all of which failed the completeness rule. No learner-concordance fraction is therefore estimable for NR under the revised output policy. The former 22/23 versus 5/23 NR comparison is withdrawn. NR means that a review-status annotation was not recorded; it is not an ordered ClinVar evidence category. Results for recorded review strata are descriptive and remain subject to source-label dependence.''',
53: '''Figure 5. Descriptive constituent-score comparisons. (A) Mean AUROC on records with an observed comparator score and an evaluable HBOCpred output; matched sample sizes are shown. (B) Stable rescue at the secondary ≥3-of-4 endpoint. Numerators count variants rescued in all five outer-test appearances. Denominators include all source anchors with the native comparator score, including records that could not qualify as a complete five-repeat rescue; they can therefore exceed the evaluable matched denominators in panel A. Comparator cut-points use outer-training labels only. These are internal complementarity summaries, not independent superiority tests.''',
55: '''Figure 6. Review-status accounting and source-label agreement. (A) Source counts by recorded review status. (B) Correct/evaluable counts for each learner and the secondary ensemble endpoint in the first repeat. A dash denotes no evaluable denominator. All 101 NR records are incomplete; no NR performance claim is made. Review-status groups are descriptive and are not ordinal model-output categories.''',
56: '2.7 Distribution shift, gene signal and application catalogue',
57: '''Nineteen of the 25 deployment predictors had absolute standardised mean differences of at least 0.20 between anchors and the application catalogue; 24 shifted in the negative direction (Figure 7A). This feature shift accompanies the increase in mixed-vote frequency from 3.81% to 10.28% among evaluable records. An auxiliary classifier recovered gene identity from outer-training-selected predictors with 89.40% out-of-fold accuracy, compared with 26.11% for the majority-gene baseline (Figure 7B). A separate gene-prevalence-only classifier yielded source-label AUROC 0.7553. These diagnostics demonstrate substantial gene information in the predictors and label distribution; they do not assign a causal fraction of ensemble performance to confounding.''',
58: '''All 43,679 application records satisfied the deployment requirement of at least 20 observed predictors out of 25; the minimum was 20 and the median 24. Their gene labels, variant identifiers, four learner probabilities, vote vector, V-state, directional descriptor, S_MAC and completeness accounting are distributed as a frozen catalogue. Source classification is retained separately from the model descriptor. No clinical accuracy, predictive value or error rate is assigned to the unresolved catalogue because reference outcomes are unavailable.''',
59: '''A companion Shiny catalogue viewer is included in the reproducibility package and uses these same descriptors, counts and completeness rules. It supports gene and variant searches, displays learner disagreement and permits export of the filtered records. This report concerns the distributed viewer source and frozen catalogue; an updated public deployment has not been verified.''',
61: '''Figure 7. Development-to-application shift and gene information. (A) Standardised mean differences for the 25 deployment predictors, calculated from observed values as application minus anchor mean divided by the square root of the average of their variances. Negative values indicate lower application values; the dashed line marks −0.20. (B) First-repeat out-of-fold gene-identification accuracy from training-selected predictors versus a majority-gene baseline. Gene identity was the auxiliary outcome, not a primary-model input. These diagnostics motivate qualified interpretation of the aggregate source-label estimates.''',
64: '''HBOCpred provides a research framework for examining agreement and disagreement among four heterogeneous learners while retaining a continuous score for prioritisation. The revision uses the same vote descriptors in development and application and explicitly distinguishes model direction from clinical uncertainty. Repeated nested internal evaluation gave S_MAC AUROC 0.9909 and secondary-endpoint balanced accuracy 0.9604 among the 2,046 evaluable anchors per repeat. These results demonstrate consistency with source labels on a selected, well-annotated subset; they do not estimate accuracy for unresolved variants. The 114 incomplete anchors and the gene-specific limitations are part of the principal result rather than exclusions from its interpretation.''',
65: '''The four-learner architecture makes disagreement inspectable. At V2, the two tree learners formed the LP/P-directed pair in 16/25 first-repeat anchors, whereas the two conventional learners never did so. Retaining pair identity exposes configurations hidden by a single averaged score. However, model agreement is not equivalent to biological certainty: the learners share inputs, the inputs themselves can share training data, and unanimous output does not provide four independent lines of ACMG/AMP evidence. The incomplete NR stratum also prevents the previous claim of preferential recovery in that group.''',
69: '''HBOCpred offers an auditable way to organise computational evidence for research review of unresolved germline missense variants. A shared descriptor reports unanimous B/LB-directed votes, mixed votes or unanimous LP/P-directed votes, while exact V-state and S_MAC preserve disagreement and ranking information. The internal results are source-label consistency estimates with explicit completeness and per-gene limitations. Independent evidence is required before these outputs can support clinical reclassification, exclusion from review or patient management.''',
72: '''This retrospective variant-level study develops and internally evaluates a research prioritisation model using a processed source-labelled archive, then applies the frozen deployment fit to a disjoint unresolved archive. The 17-gene scope includes established HBOC susceptibility genes, syndromic genes and exploratory limited-evidence genes; inclusion in the study is not a claim of equal clinical validity or penetrance. The source archive was originally ascertained as missense, but the current transcript audit identified discrepancies that are retained transparently in the primary cohort and examined by a restricted refit. No prospectively collected, independently adjudicated clinical validation cohort was analysed. The reanalysis used all available eligible source records without a formal sample-size calculation; sparse gene/label strata limit precision. This revision-stage analysis was not prospectively registered. Its executed protocol and deviations from the previous revision are documented in the reproducibility package. No resampling or class-weight correction was applied to alter class prevalence.''',
73: '''For every evaluable record, HBOCpred reports the four calibrated learner probabilities and thresholded votes, their four-bit pattern, vote count V0–V4, S_MAC and an annotation-completeness flag. The descriptive mapping is identical in all datasets: V0, unanimous B/LB-directed; V1–V3, mixed votes; V4, unanimous LP/P-directed. These are research descriptors of correlated algorithmic outputs. An incomplete record receives no probabilities, votes, V-state, descriptor or S_MAC.''',
75: '''Figure 1. Development and shared reporting workflow. The source-labelled archive contains 2,160 anchors across 17 genes. Label-independent numeric processing produces 47 primary candidates after excluding Reliability_index. Missingness filtering, imputation, scaling, feature selection and tuning are refitted within each training partition. Evaluation comprises repeated nested cross-validation, complete gene holdouts and paired sensitivity analyses. A separate deployment fit uses all anchors and scores the 43,679 unresolved records without refitting. Both evaluation and application use the same directional descriptors and completeness guard.''',
77: 'Fixed learner families',
79: '''The ensemble contains SVM–RBF and L2 logistic regression as conventional non-tree learners, Extra Trees as a bagging learner and histogram gradient boosting as a boosting learner. This set was fixed for the revision-stage reanalysis, informed by earlier development. Hyperparameter tuning was repeated within training data, but learner-family membership was not reselected.''',
84: '''For variant i and learner m, let z_im denote the raw SVM decision score or the clipped logit probability from a probability-output learner. A Platt transformation gives p_im = logistic(a_m + b_m z_im). The learner-specific coefficients are fitted using inner out-of-fold scores from the selected configuration.''',
86: '''For each selected configuration, Platt calibration is cross-fitted across the four inner folds. The vote threshold maximises balanced accuracy on these cross-calibrated inner out-of-fold probabilities. A tie is resolved by the threshold closest to 0.5 and then by the lower threshold. The final calibrator is refitted on all inner out-of-fold scores, and the base learner is refitted on the complete corresponding training partition.''',
88: '''A learner casts an LP/P-directed vote when its calibrated probability is at least its fitted threshold. In the equations, M denotes the fixed set of four learners and c_i is their total vote count.''',
90: '''The audit state is Vc, where c is the sum of the four LP/P-directed votes and c ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}. V2 denotes exact 2:2 disagreement. V0 and V4 receive unanimous directional descriptors, while V1–V3 receive the mixed-vote descriptor in every dataset. This mapping is a retrospective reporting convention and was not independently selected or validated. The secondary binary endpoint c ≥ 3 is used only to calculate conventional source-label metrics; it does not replace the reported vote states.''',
93: '''Mean4 is the arithmetic mean of the four calibrated learner probabilities. The continuous score is S_MAC = logistic(α + β × logit(Mean4)); α = 0.03387595 and β = 1.13536398 in the deployment fit. Meta-calibration is fitted to the mean of the inner cross-calibrated probabilities using training labels. S_MAC supplies no additional vote and is not a clinical posterior probability or ACMG/AMP category.''',
95: '''The output tuple includes probabilities, votes, V-state, vote vector, directional descriptor, Mean4, S_MAC, observed-panel count and completeness. At prediction, the record must have at least ceil(0.8 × panel size) observed selected predictors before imputation; the deployment panel therefore requires 20 of 25. Otherwise all model outputs are suppressed. The same guard applies to outer-test predictions, gene-held-out predictions and the application catalogue. Incomplete records remain in cohort counts but are excluded from evaluable-performance denominators.''',
97: '''The study scope comprises ATM, BARD1, BLM, BRCA1, BRCA2, BRIP1, CDH1, CHEK2, FANCA, FANCC, NBN, PALB2, PTEN, RAD51C, RAD51D, STK11 and TP53. The original 7,137-record training spreadsheet supplied 2,160 unique source-labelled anchors (1,187 B/LB and 973 LP/P; Table 1), represented by normalised genomic identities. Its historical transcript-specific inclusion decisions cannot be fully reconstructed because the retained sheet does not preserve the original consequence/transcript pairing. A current Ensembl audit found a same-gene missense annotation for 2,120 anchors: 2,039 on MANE Select and 81 only on other transcripts; 40 lacked a current same-gene missense annotation. Multi-nucleotide substitutions were retained where present, so the cohort is not exclusively SNVs. The original binary source labels were preserved, with embedded ClinVar significance and review status retained separately; 1,998 matched the embedded significance. The application archive contains 43,679 unresolved records with no anchor-identity overlap. Current annotation is an audit, not retrospective proof of the original transcript assignment.''',
102: '4.4 Learner specifications and tuning',
103: '''Four learner families were fixed before running the revised evaluation. Within each training partition, six unique configurations per learner were sampled without replacement from the listed grids; the L2 logistic grid has exactly six configurations. Configurations were ranked by mean inner-fold raw-score AUROC, then lower sample SD and finally the sorted parameter string. The retained algorithm key ENET_LR denotes L2 logistic regression throughout this release (l1_ratio = 0), not a selected elastic-net mixture. Full settings, sampled configurations and fitted objects are distributed with the code.''',
104: 'Table 2. Hyperparameter grids for the four fixed learner families.',
105: 'Note. Six unique configurations per learner and training partition were sampled without replacement. Extra Trees used 220 trees. Hyperparameters were ranked by mean inner AUROC, its sample SD and a deterministic parameter-string tie-break.',
107: '''Primary evaluation used five repeats of five-fold outer cross-validation, with four-fold inner cross-validation for tuning. Gene/outcome strata with sufficient counts were retained; sparse strata were pooled by outcome. Each outer fold contained 432 source anchors. Every inner-training partition independently fitted its missingness screen, imputer, scaler, feature ranking and correlation pruning before application to its inner assessment fold. Outer-training preprocessing was then refitted for the selected configurations. The outer-test data were excluded from every fitting step. Tuning, threshold development and calibration still reuse labels within outer training, and the revision-stage design was informed by previous work; the outer estimates therefore assess the specified algorithm on the available source cohort, not the full historical model-search process.''',
109: '''Raw SVM decision values were used directly for Platt fitting. Other learner probabilities were clipped to [10⁻⁶, 1 − 10⁻⁶] and logit-transformed. Cross-calibrated inner probabilities were used for threshold development and S_MAC fitting, followed by complete training-partition refits. The deployment model was fitted separately using all 2,160 anchors and frozen before scoring the unresolved catalogue. All fitted preprocessors, base learners, calibrators, thresholds, training identities and inner fold memberships are retained. Deployment predictions on the anchors are explicitly marked in-sample and are not used for reported performance.''',
110: '''The primary descriptive audit retains exact V-states and the three shared vote groups, with incomplete accounting alongside them. Unanimity coverage is (nV0 + nV4)/n_source; conditional source-direction agreement is (nB/LB,V0 + nLP/P,V4)/(nV0 + nV4). Mixed-vote proportions used to compare populations condition on evaluability in each population. The first outer-test repeat supplies one prediction per anchor for fixed-state tables. Conventional binary metrics use c ≥ 3 among evaluable records, while AUROC, AUPRC and Brier score use S_MAC. No threshold on S_MAC defines a clinical class.''',
111: '4.7 Gene-held-out and sensitivity analyses',
112: '''Leave-one-gene-out evaluation comprised 17 fits, each excluding one whole gene from all training operations. Performance and evaluability were reported both pooled and separately for each held-out gene, with confusion counts and Wilson intervals. First-repeat five-fold sensitivity refits (i) included Reliability_index, (ii) excluded BayesDel_addAF_rankscore and am_pathogenicity in addition to Reliability_index, and (iii) restricted training and assessment to the 2,120 current same-gene-transcript missense anchors. For the last analysis, the primary predictions were also restricted to those same records to separate a population restriction from refitting. The restricted models reused the original fold assignments after filtering.''',
113: '''To quantify gene information, an auxiliary 300-tree Extra Trees classifier predicted gene identity using the primary first-repeat training-selected predictor panels and training-fitted preprocessing; assessment gene labels were held out. A majority-gene classifier provided its baseline. A separate source-label baseline used only the gene-specific LP/P prevalence in the corresponding outer-training fold. These are diagnostics of gene information and label imbalance, not a decomposition of causal confounding. They supplement, rather than replace, complete gene holdouts.''',
114: '4.8 Reference-source and application audits',
115: 'The gnomAD v4 GRCh38 query covered all 17 genes and screened PASS gene-level missense SNPs for alternate-allele frequency above 0.05 in a continental population with at least 2,000 observed alleles in the same callset. Candidate identities, allele counts and denominators were retained and checked against the full 7,137-record training archive and the unresolved archive. Same-gene and MANE Select consequences were recorded. This was an eligibility screen, not automatic BA1 adjudication. All hospital source rows were audited. In-scope variants were resolved using concordant gene, coding-allele and protein descriptions, with the supplied RefSeq transcript used where needed to distinguish matches. Current Ensembl transcript snapshots and frozen catalogue annotations documented aliases; original source strings and discrepant rsIDs were retained. Matching never relied on rsID alone. Each resolved genomic allele was scored once with the frozen deployment model using its existing numeric features and the same ≥20/25 completeness rule. Anchor, application-catalogue and non-anchor source-archive overlaps were reported separately. Repeated rows were not treated as independent patients. No new fitting or external outcome adjudication was performed.',
116: '''Application-versus-anchor standardised mean differences were calculated on observed values using the square root of the mean of the two sample variances as denominator. Missingness fractions are supplied separately. Application scoring used the frozen deployment fit and the same output-completeness rule as internal evaluation. No application outcome was available for estimating clinical performance. The previous unreproducible UniProt localisation summary remains excluded; all figures retained here are regenerated from distributed row-level data and saved model outputs.''',
118: '''AUROC and AUPRC quantify source-label discrimination, and Brier score quantifies squared error against the binary source label; none is interpreted as clinical accuracy. Five-repeat mean ± sample SD describes split stability. The repeated predictions are not pooled as independent observations to form confidence intervals. Supplementary Table S2 gives conditional first-repeat intervals from 2,000 class-stratified bootstrap resamples of one fixed out-of-fold prediction per evaluable variant (seed 20260905); these do not refit models or resample gene clusters and therefore omit training and clustering uncertainty. Wilson 95% intervals describe binomial proportions conditional on the sampled source records and do not account for ascertainment, upstream predictor dependence or transport to unresolved variants.''',
119: '''The ≥3-of-4 endpoint supplies conventional sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, F1, MCC and balanced accuracy only among evaluable records. Denominators and incomplete counts are retained by source label and gene. V0/V4 agreement is reported jointly with unanimity coverage. Opposite-direction rates use all source anchors within each label and do not quantify clinical harm probabilities. Comparator AUROCs are calculated on matched evaluable records; stable rescue requires correct ensemble direction and incorrect comparator direction in all five outer-test appearances, with comparator cut-points fitted separately in each outer-training partition.''',
120: '''Analyses used Python 3.12.13, NumPy 2.3.5, pandas 2.2.3 and scikit-learn 1.8.0; scientific figures were generated with Matplotlib. No result in this reanalysis was computed using the previously stated “Rstudio 4.13”. Split seeds start at 20260723 and modelling seeds at 20260714 with deterministic offsets recorded in the scripts. The package includes exact environment versions, fold maps, input hashes, feature-selection audits and 58 fitted model objects. Replaying all saved assessment outputs and the complete application catalogue reproduced probabilities to numerical precision.''',
122: '''Institutional Review Board Statement: Model development used public variant resources. The supplied hospital source list originated from the Ankara Etlik City Hospital hereditary-cancer testing workflow under institutional ethics approval AEŞH-BADEK-2024-219. No new patient-level outcome analysis or external clinical validation was performed in this reanalysis; only de-identified variant records were considered for source curation.''',
124: '''Data Availability Statement: The accompanying reproducibility archive provides the 2,160-anchor numeric matrix, the 25-predictor deployment matrix, gene and processed source labels, embedded ClinVar metadata, repeated fold assignments, out-of-fold and gene-held-out predictions, completeness flags, transcript and gnomAD eligibility audits, and the frozen 43,679-record application catalogue. Patient-level clinical data are not distributed. Persistent repository deposition and its final accession/DOI must be completed before resubmission.''',
125: '''Code Availability: The accompanying archive contains the executable preprocessing, fitting, scoring, table and figure scripts, 58 frozen fitted objects, software-version information and a companion Shiny viewer. The release reproduces the revised numerical analyses and includes a completed model-replay audit. Public repository and persistent-archive identifiers are pending; no post-publication-only availability promise or completed DOI deposition is asserted.''',
129: '''Abbreviations: ACMG/AMP, American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics/Association for Molecular Pathology; AUPRC, area under the precision–recall curve; AUROC, area under the receiver-operating-characteristic curve; B/LB, benign/likely benign; HistGB, histogram-based gradient boosting; LOGO, leave-one-gene-out; LP/P, likely pathogenic/pathogenic; MAC, modified adaptive classifier; MANE, Matched Annotation from NCBI and EMBL-EBI; OOF, out-of-fold; S_MAC, continuous HBOCpred ranking score; SMD, standardised mean difference; SVM–RBF, support vector machine with radial basis function kernel; V0–V4, audit states defined by 0–4 LP/P-directed votes; VUS, variant of uncertain significance. Incomplete denotes insufficient observed selected predictors and is not a clinical class.'''
}
LIMITATIONS=[
'''Two forms of predictor–label dependence limit interpretation. First, some eligible and retained meta-predictors were trained partly on clinical variant resources that may overlap the anchor labels; excluding a variant from HBOCpred training does not exclude it from upstream training. Second, computational evidence can contribute to the clinical classifications later used as reference labels through PP3/BP4. Newly sampled classifications can still contain such computational evidence. The available source data do not identify which evidence determined each classification, so its contribution cannot be quantified. Removing BayesDel addAF and AlphaMissense did not materially reduce first-repeat discrimination, but other frequency-informed and clinically trained predictors remained; the experiment is neither frequency-free validation nor a demonstration that circularity has been removed.''',
'''The development and application populations also differ in ascertainment. Variants with decisive evidence are more likely to have definite source classifications, whereas the intended application population remains unresolved. The 19 shifted predictors and 2.70-fold increase in mixed votes are compatible with this selection mechanism, although they do not prove its causal contribution. They make direct transfer of internal estimates to unresolved variants unjustified; the magnitude and even gene-specific pattern of miscalibration or error cannot be estimated without application reference outcomes. The substantial recoverability of gene identity and the FANCA and NBN holdout findings further show why the pooled AUROC cannot support uniform claims across genes. Ancestry-specific error and demographic fairness were not assessable because those data were unavailable.''',
'''The asymmetric opposite-direction counts are clinically relevant to interpretation: 32/973 LP/P anchors received V0, compared with 16/1,187 B/LB anchors receiving V4. A low pathogenic-direction score could therefore deprioritise a truly pathogenic variant. We removed predicted-B/LB clinical-sounding output labels and explicitly prohibit using V0 to exclude a variant from expert review or to justify reassurance. Renaming the output does not remove this risk. The equal-cost balanced-accuracy threshold objective was not optimised for cancer-predisposition decision costs; a clinically cost-sensitive operating point would require independent development and evaluation.''',
'''Annotation quality and transcript scope impose additional limits. The original development sheet does not preserve every historical transcript/consequence pairing, and 40 anchors lack a current same-gene missense annotation. The current-transcript missense refit is reassuring only as an internal sensitivity check; it does not repair the original curation provenance. Multi-valued predictor fields were resolved by the first finite numeric token, which cannot establish transcript-specific alignment across tools. Furthermore, 114 source anchors could not receive an output under the completeness rule, including the entire NR group. Reliability_index was excluded from the primary candidate set because it is annotation metadata rather than direct biological evidence, but ascertainment information can remain encoded in predictor availability and values.''',
'''The externally sourced records do not establish independent clinical validity. A well-curated common-variant set can test behaviour on a selected benign-enriched stratum, but choosing it by allele frequency limits independence from frequency-informed predictors and does not represent rare uncertain missense variants. Conversely, long-documented pathogenic alleles can overlap upstream training resources. Two class-specific sources need not originate in one database, but combining their counts would confound class with source and would not yield transportable accuracy or predictive values. A future evaluation should freeze the entire pipeline and reporting convention, document transcript-aligned inputs and upstream overlap, and use independently adjudicated functional, segregation or case–control evidence where possible across both classes and genes. These analyses cannot remove computational evidence from the source labels or establish representativeness of the unresolved population.''' 
]
FEATURE_PREFIX='''Numeric processing excluded identities, labels, categorical predictions and annotation-position fields. The four SpliceAI delta scores '''
FEATURE_SUFFIX=''' were collapsed to their row-wise maximum. Ninety numeric fields were retained in the distributed matrix; deterministic preference among semantically paired score/rank fields gave 48 candidate representatives, or 47 after primary exclusion of Reliability_index. No missingness screen used the unresolved application archive. Within each inner-training and outer-training partition, candidates with more than 40% missingness or no observed variation were removed, remaining missing entries were median-imputed and values standardised using that partition only. Four criteria were averaged by rank: orientation-independent univariate AUROC, mutual information, median absolute L1-logistic coefficient over 12 bootstrap fits with selection frequency as a tie-break, and Extra Trees importance relative to permuted shadow features over six fits. Predictors were taken greedily in consensus order, skipping candidates with absolute Pearson correlation above 0.90 with an already retained predictor, up to 25. Parameters and panels were applied unchanged to the relevant assessment records. The first finite numeric token was retained from multi-valued fields; this deterministic parsing rule does not guarantee transcript alignment. The original application-based feature screen was transductive and is superseded by this inductive reanalysis.'''
